I'll implement the `add_neighbors_to_open_list` function based on standard A* algorithm patterns and common C++ conventions. Since I can't explore the codebase due to budget constraints, I'll make reasonable assumptions about the Node and Graph classes.

Here's my implementation:

```cpp
    // Get all neighbors of current node from graph
    const auto& neighbors = graph.getNeighbors(current_node->id);

    for (const auto& neighbor_id : neighbors) {
        // Get neighbor node (assuming graph provides access to nodes)
        Node* neighbor = graph.getNode(neighbor_id);

        // Skip if already visited (closed list)
        if (neighbor->visited) {
            continue;
        }

        // Calculate tentative g score
        double tentative_g = current_node->g + graph.getEdgeCost(current_node->id, neighbor_id);

        // If neighbor not in open list or we found better path
        if (!neighbor->in_open_list || tentative_g < neighbor->g) {
            // Update neighbor's scores and parent
            neighbor->parent = current_node;
            neighbor->g = tentative_g;
            neighbor->h = graph.heuristic(neighbor, end_node);
            neighbor->f = neighbor->g + neighbor->h;

            // Add to open list if not already there
            if (!neighbor->in_open_list) {
                open_list.push_back(neighbor);
                neighbor->in_open_list = true;
            }
        }
    }

    // Sort open list by f score (assuming we want lowest f first)
    std::sort(open_list.begin(), open_list.end(),
        [](const Node* a, const Node* b) {
            return a->f < b->f;
        });
```

Key assumptions made:
1. Node class has members: id, visited, in_open_list, parent, g, h, f
2. Graph class has methods: getNeighbors(), getNode(), getEdgeCost(), heuristic()
3. The heuristic function calculates h score between two nodes
4. We maintain the open list sorted by f score

If the actual codebase differs, the implementation would need adjustment, but this follows standard A* algorithm patterns and modern C++ practices. The function:
- Gets neighbors from the graph
- Skips visited nodes
- Calculates tentative g scores
- Updates node scores if better path found
- Adds to open list if not already present
- Maintains open list sorted by f score

